This course will cover four fundamental scenarios in game theory:


simultaneous-move game under complete information;

sequential-move game under complete information;

simultaneous-move game under incomplete information;

sequential-move game under incomplete information.


The focus of the course will mainly be applied, although some general principles will be discussed in order to address the strategic situations more rigorously.

Different examples, ranging from daily life and business fields, will show the importance and usefulness of the concepts discussed in class.

The main goal of this course is to develop your skills to employ game theory to examine interactive decision problems, i.e., situations whereby every agent’s actions have payoff consequences on other agents. In particular, by the end of this course, you will be able to:


analyze the available strategies for each decision-maker;

solve related equilibria;

predict and describe how a game is going to be played by the players (i.e., their equilibrium behavior);

construct the structure of interactive decision problems;

model economics and business topics covered in other courses using game theory.

